## 10. Public Modified Versions and Forks

You may publicly distribute a Modified Version only if all of the following conditions are satisfied:

1. the Modified Version is distributed without charge;
2. the complete Corresponding Source of the mau-derived portions is publicly available without charge;
3. the mau-derived portions remain under the mau License 1.0;
4. a complete copy of this License is included;
5. original copyright and attribution notices are preserved;
6. the Modified Version clearly states that modifications were made;
7. the Modified Version has its own independent name as required by Section 13;
8. it clearly identifies itself as an unofficial independent version;
9. its main README or equivalent primary documentation prominently states "Based on mau" or equivalent wording;
10. it does not imply that it is official, approved, maintained, sponsored, or endorsed by HexedMaya or the official mau project;
11. it is not offered in a manner constituting Software Monetization.

A public fork may compete with mau.

Competition itself is not prohibited.

## 11. Corresponding Source for Distributed Modified Versions

If you distribute a Modified Version, the complete Corresponding Source for the mau-derived portions of that exact version must be publicly available.

"Corresponding Source" means the source code in the preferred form for making modifications, together with files reasonably necessary to build and modify the mau-derived portions.

Corresponding Source must:

* be available without payment;
* be reasonably accessible;
* correspond to the distributed version;
* remain under this License.

You are not required to publish unrelated proprietary code merely because it interoperates with or uses mau.

If a Modified Runtime is distributed as part of Your Product, only the source of the mau-derived Modified Runtime is subject to this Section. The independent source code of Your Product does not become subject to this License.

## 12. Attribution

You may not:

* remove original mau copyright notices from the Software;
* falsely claim authorship of mau code;
* hide the origin of a public Modified Version;
* remove contributor attribution required by the version from which your Modified Version derives.

Ordinary production transformations such as minification, bundling, compression, or compilation are permitted.

If such a process removes an attribution notice from Runtime or Generated Output, equivalent attribution may instead be preserved in a reasonably accessible notices, legal, license, metadata, documentation, or source-map location distributed with Your Product.

No visible end-user badge is required solely because Your Product uses mau.

## 13. Name, Project Identity, and Branding

This License does not grant a general right to use the names:

* "mau";
* "Make A UI";

the `.mau` project identity, official mau logos, or other official mau branding as the name or primary identity of an independent product, fork, service, package, repository, organization, or domain.

You may truthfully state:

* "Built with mau";
* "Powered by mau";
* "Uses mau";
* "Based on mau";
* "Compatible with mau";

where factually accurate.

You may use the name mau when reasonably necessary to describe compatibility, origin, dependency, or factual comparison.

You may not use mau branding in a way that suggests official status, ownership, approval, sponsorship, partnership, certification, or endorsement where none exists.

A Modified Version must use its own independent project name.

Without prior written permission, an independent Modified Version may not use names such as:

* mau Pro;
* mau Plus;
* Better mau;
* New mau;
* mau 2;
* mau Next;
* mau Enhanced;
* mau Ultimate;
* Make A UI Plus;

or another name that uses mau's identity to present the independent version as the official, improved, premium, newer, replacement, or successor version of mau.

Truthful wording such as:

"CatUI — based on mau"

is permitted.

Nothing in this Section claims or creates trademark, design, name, or other rights that do not otherwise exist under applicable law.

## 14. Mau Attribution Notice in Generated Output

The Compiler may place a Mau Attribution Notice in Generated Output.

The notice may be a source-code comment or other non-user-facing technical marker.

You may transform, bundle, or minify Generated Output.

If ordinary build tooling removes the original notice, you must preserve equivalent mau attribution in another reasonably accessible technical or legal notice distributed with Your Product.

You are not required to:

* display a visible "Built with mau" badge;
* place mau branding in your user interface;
* advertise mau to your customers.

You may voluntarily do so if the use complies with Section 13.

